# Changelog

Convoy detection over 275.9 M traffic records (plate, location, timestamp). Spark standalone on Slurm-managed containers, data on GPFS.

Newest first. Each entry: what changed, and — when not obvious — why.


## 2026-05-21

- Removed `.lede { max-width: 720px }` from both bright (`in-process.css`) and neon (`final.css`) stylesheets, plus one inline `max-width: 820px` on climax's hero lede. Prose now flows to the section-inner column width (1100 px), so wrapping happens at the page edge rather than at a typographic-ideal-but-narrower cap. This addresses "text doesn't touch the line end" on the bright pages.

- HTML prose un-wrapped on the three new pages (`sviluppo.html`, `climax.html`, `final.html`): the no-hard-wrap rule applies to rendered prose anywhere, not just markdown. 14 paragraphs across `<p>`, `<li>`, `<figcaption>`, and one stray multi-line `<span>` collapsed to single logical lines. Tables, lists-as-list, and code blocks left untouched (they need real line breaks).

- Climax slide 2 (Algorithm) — pseudocode `.algo` code block removed; replaced by tighter prose covering the local-pruning insight and the inverted-index dominance check. Frees up roughly half the slide's vertical space.

- ←/→ cross-section navigation at slide boundaries. Each page's `<body>` carries `data-prev` and `data-next` attributes pointing at neighbour-section URLs (chain: index → sviluppo → climax → final). At *either* boundary slide of a section (first OR last), **both** ← and → trigger cross-section navigation — ← → previous section, → → next section. Mid-section the arrows no-op. The existing ←/→ horizontal scroll on Inizio's Algorithm-timeline section is preserved via an early-return that fires before the boundary check.

- Demo site finalised at **4 acts with Italian nav**: Inizio · Sviluppo · Climax · Conclusione. `pages/sviluppo.html` (NEW, bright theme) restores the full FP-Growth reproduction narrative — 8 snap-scroll slides, hero is Shadow Convoy + 4 stats, last slide is the bridge-to-MaxGrowth ("cells → routes" annotation). `pages/climax.html` (bright theme) reduced to **3 slides only**: Shadow Trajectory title (subtitle "cells → routes"), Algorithm explanation (m·k·d·ε + 8-stage pipeline + pseudocode + local-pruning insight), To Be Completed (status banner + open-items table). `pages/final.html` (neon theme) is now a **single-slide placeholder**: "To Be Continued" with three labelled coming-soon columns (Results · Analysis · Conclusion) and a pulsing "awaiting validation" badge. `pages/index.html` (Inizio, dark) is unchanged in content, only the nav updated to 4 tabs.

- Earlier same day: 3-act collapse (Inizio · Climax · Fine). (Beginning / The Algorithm / Conclusion). `pages/in-process.html` deleted. `climax.html` rewritten as the MaxGrowth-algorithm explanation (no results — pipeline-built but day-1 smoke still tuning; ends with an honest "implementation complete, validation pending" banner). `final.html` rewritten as a comprehensive conclusion: what we built (substrate + FP-Growth + MaxGrowth), what we found (Fleets A/B/D + the OCR ghost), what's pending (full-month MaxGrowth + 3 other items), distilled lessons. Bright theme gained MaxGrowth-specific classes (`.param-grid`, `.algo`, `.failure-table`, `.status-banner`, Spark/driver-tinted `.step` variants) so the climax page renders the algorithm content properly. `index.html` (`Inizio`) is the dark-theme Shadow Convoy title page unchanged in content; only the nav was updated to the new 3-tab Italian structure.

- Earlier same day: 4-act arc with Chinese nav (开端 / 发展 / 高潮 / 结局). Top nav now carries Chinese act-names + small English subtitles; tab heights bumped to 64 px to fit two-line labels. The bright theme was rebuilt to be properly cyberpunk — porting the dark page's glitch effects, scanlines, neon text-shadows, and corner-bracket boxes onto a brighter cream palette with saturated electric blue and hot pink-red accents. Two new pages: `pages/climax.html` (the analytical-findings narrative — OCR catch, the 1,548-tie, Fleet D's true 16-plate size, super-connector chart) reuses `in-process.css`; `pages/final.html` rewritten from a stub into a 7-slide snap-scroll page covering the MaxGrowth methods (problem shift, 4 parameters, 8-stage pipeline, growth recursion with local-pruning trick, four failure modes, three forward-work directions). `pages/css/final.css` rebuilt accordingly with rainbow-sweep title, glitch effects, parameter cards, pipeline steps with Spark/driver colour coding, failure table, and matching nav-dot/progress-bar styling.



- `doc/MaxGrowth.md`: full pipeline reference (problem statement, parameters, 8-stage pipeline diagram, growth recursion walkthrough, inverted-index maximality filter, distribution/broadcast envelope, the four failure modes we hit and resolved, tuning knobs by symptom, remaining unbuilt extensions). Companion to `src/max_growth/README.md` (which stays as the developer's quick-start) and `doc/Methods.md` (which stays as the academic context).

- MaxGrowth day1 log.4: Spark stages succeeded, then the driver-side `maximal_only()` got stuck on **2.62 M raw patterns** (O(N²) = 6.9 T comparisons ≈ ~19 h per single core). Two algorithmic fixes: • `grow_from_root` now applies **local maximality pruning** — skip emission at depth L if any extension to L+1 preserves the full current membership. Cuts raw emissions ~10–100× by removing sub-patterns that will be dominated by their own children. • `maximal_only` rewritten with an **inverted index** (plate → patterns containing it), seeded on the rarest member of each pattern. Brings per-pattern dominance check from O(N) to O(|candidates|), typically a few dozen. Plus `--skip-maximal-filter` flag for time-constrained runs when raw pattern count is still excessive.


## 2026-05-20

- MaxGrowth third failure: `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'core'` on every executor. Driver-side `sys.path.insert(...)` only fixes the driver; cloudpickle serializes `grow_from_root` by reference to module `core`, and executors couldn't resolve it. Fix: `sc.addPyFile(.../core.py)` after `getOrCreate()`. Architecture validated through cluster materialization (199,949 plates / 3.5 M visits / 293,195 ε-clusters in ~40 s).

- MaxGrowth refactored to distributed enumeration. `core.py` now exposes `grow_from_root(root, by_camera, pos, next_cams, m, k, d)` — single-root recursion as a top-level function. `max_growth_job.py` parallelizes it across Spark executors via broadcast-then-map: trajectories, position indices, and clusters-by-camera are broadcast (≲ 1 GB total after prefilter); the cluster-root list is `sc.parallelize`'d into 200 slices; each task runs `grow_from_root` on its slice and yields patterns; Spark `.distinct()` dedupes; maximality filter runs on the (now small) collected pattern set on driver. Cluster computation also moved to Spark: dual-offset bucketize per camera (offset 0 and ε/2), `--max-cluster-size 500` drops pathological mega- clusters analogously to FP-Growth's `density_cap`. Result: `--max-plates` raised from 20k to 500k by default; ~90× speedup on the growth phase from cluster parallelism (5 nodes × 18 cores).

- MaxGrowth second failure: day1 finished the Spark stage in seconds but the driver got OOM-killed (exit 137) after 2 h 42 min of driver-side enumeration on **932,235 plates / 7.17 M visits**. The paper-safe `--min-visits=k=3` Apriori prefilter is wrong for ANPR-scale data — at 10⁶ vehicles/day, almost every regular commuter visits ≥ 3 cameras. Fix: added `--min-observations` (default 10) and `--max-plates` (default 20,000) as additional prefilters. Mirrors FP-Growth's `min_count` semantics; bounds the driver collect. Architectural note: if the cap-by-frequency approach is too lossy for the final paper, the next step is distributing the `growth()` recursion across Spark partitions rather than running it on the driver.


## 2026-05-19

- MaxGrowth first 31-day run hit `spark.driver.maxResultSize` (1 GB default) when collect()ing trajectories to the driver — collected size 1,046 MiB with the paper-safe `--min-visits=k=3` prefilter, which barely prunes on 31-day-scale data. Fix: set `spark.driver.maxResultSize=0` in the job's SparkSession config. The real bound is now driver heap (32 GB). For full-month runs, raising `--min-visits` to 10–30 is the principled second knob — drops the trajectory-collect size by orders of magnitude at the cost of dropping occasional-visitor platoons.

- `src/max_growth/` (`core.py`, `max_growth_job.py`, `README.md`, `__init__.py`): PySpark implementation of MaxGrowth (Bei et al., VLDB 2024). Submitted via the existing `cluster.sbatch` with `JOB=…/max_growth_job.py` — no Slurm or Spark plumbing changes needed. Pre-filters plates with < k visits (Apriori- safe), builds trajectories distributively in Spark, then runs the recursive `growth()` enumeration on the driver after the Apriori cut. Verified on a synthetic 4-plate fixture: produces correct platoon `{1,2,3}` on a 3-camera route, and demonstrates `d=1` gap-tolerance recovering a 3-plate platoon that strict-consecutive matching would lose.

- `scripts/ocr_canonicalize.py`: edit-1 substitution scan over plates that appear in the convoy CSV; flags candidate OCR-confusion pairs (e.g. 16970573 vs 16972992) for manual canonicalization. Runs in seconds.

- `scripts/graph_communities.py`: build a weighted plate co-occurrence graph from the convoy CSV (size-k itemsets unrolled to C(k,2) edges, weights summed), run Louvain (fallback: Label Propagation) via networkx, emit `communities.csv` (per-plate) and `fleets.csv` (per-community). Promotes the manual Fleet A / B / D inspection into systematic labels.

- `scripts/requirements.txt`: + `networkx`, `pandas`, `matplotlib` for the graph and analysis scripts.

- `doc/Methods.md`: literature review of co-movement / convoy / platoon pattern mining from 2006 (Flock) → 2024 (MaxGrowth, VLDB) → 2025 (Flink streaming). Names our problem precisely as "checkpoint frequent co-occurrence pattern (FCP) / platoon mining", explains why our FP-Growth baseline is a 2015-era approach, identifies SOTA (MaxGrowth) and recommends a three-step forward plan: (1) OCR-noise plate canonicalization, (2) graph + community detection over existing output, (3) PySpark MaxGrowth as the ambitious target.

- `PLAN.md` moved to `doc/PLAN.md` to consolidate project documentation.


## 2026-05-18

- Demo site grew a cross-page navigator: `pages/index.html` (Start, dark theme — unchanged content), `pages/in-process.html` (bright theme — mid-term reproduction report), `pages/final.html` (neon theme — stub for end-of-term deliverable). Three theme palettes are co-declared in `pages/css/nav.css` and scoped via `[data-theme]` attribute selectors. Tab hover sweeps a two-stop gradient in the **destination theme's own palette** — each tab shows its own theme regardless of where you are.

- In Process page is now snap-scroll, page-like in the same shape as Start: 8 viewport-sized `<section>`s under `#page-wrapper`, right-side dot nav, top scanline + bottom progress bar, slim self-contained `pages/js/in-process.js` for the controller and the §4 top-5 tab switcher.

- **Full-month convoy run succeeded.** 4 h 38 min wall time, 5,258,677 transactions, min_count=50, **5,447 convoys** over 31 days. Some transient `heartbeat timed out` warnings (Spark recovered via retry, no fatal errors).

- `scripts/analyze_convoys.py`: summary + three PNG charts (size, count, super-connectors). Key findings: • Only 783 distinct plates of 22 M ever appear in any convoy (0.0036%); convoy signal is extraordinarily thin. • Top single pair `(477634, 491688)` co-located 628× in 31 days = 20.3×/day. • Four plates `{3271142, 16972992, 16970573, 511670}` appear in *exactly* 1,548 convoys each — i.e. they are always observed together. Strongest candidate for a real 4+ car fleet operating as a unit. • Top-5 at k=5 and k=6 are the same fleet ± one member, confirming a permanent 6-car convoy `{16970573, 16972992, 18150551, 3271142, 3542309, 511670}`.


## 2026-05-16

- Week-1 convoy run **succeeded**: 61 min, 41,509 convoys at `min_count=10`, 1.17 M transactions. No errors, no executor losses, no retries. Size distribution is inverted-U (22.7 k pairs → 2.9 k triples → 7.1 k k=6), fingerprint of structured fleets — small groups travelling as a whole generating combinatorially many subset-itemsets. Top single pair `(1737487, 3279908)` co-located 243× in 7 days (~35×/day), plate `3279908` is in 9 of the top 13 convoys → likely paired service or commercial vehicles. Full-month projection (recalibrated): 4-8 h at `--min-count 10`, or 1-3 h at span-matched `--min-count 70`.


## 2026-05-15

- Day-1 convoy run **succeeded**: 4 min, 850 size-2 convoys, top pair `(323390, 420190)` co-located 25× in 24 h. All three 2026-05-14 fixes (density_cap, -Xss16m, no df.persist) held — no retries, no executor losses.

- `scripts/slice_range.sh` (replaces `slice_day.sh`): generalized to any day range. Produced `week1.csv` = days 1-7 = 66.9 M rows / 1.4 GB in 69 s. Why: a 7-day slice gives an intermediate scale point between day1's smoke test (~50 k transactions) and the full month (~1.5 M), so any genuinely scale-dependent failure surfaces in ~20 min instead of ~2 h.


## 2026-05-14

- `scripts/slice_day.sh`: awk one-liner to extract a 24-hour slice from the 31-day CSV. Day-1 slice = 9.28 M rows / 208 MB; produced in ~1 min. Why: convoy job iteration was costing ~8 h per failed attempt. With the slice it's 1-2 min, a 240× speedup on debug-loop length.

- `convoy_fpgrowth.py`: removed `df.persist()` for the raw input; raised `spark.network.timeout` to 600 s, `spark.executor.heartbeatInterval` to 30 s. Why: third failure mode — all 5 executors hit heartbeat timeout (125-172 s silent) after FP-Growth + 3 overlapping persists overcommitted heap. Cached `df` was the worst offender (~13 GB decompressed across cluster) for ~zero benefit, since each grid does one scan and GPFS reads are fast. Removing it gives FP-Growth back its heap headroom; the longer timeout is insurance for genuinely long FP-tree builds.

- `convoy_fpgrowth.py`: set `-Xss16m` on executor + driver JVMs. Why: with density_cap=2000 the cgroup OOM was gone, but a new failure appeared — `java.lang.StackOverflowError` inside Kryo serialization of FP-trees (`Tuple2Serializer.write` ↔ `TraversableSerializer.write` recursion, ~2000 frames deep). FP-tree path depth scales with transaction width; the default 1 MB thread stack can't hold 2000-deep recursive serialization. 16 MB ≈ 16k-deep margin at negligible memory cost (18 cores × 16 MB = 288 MB per executor vs. 75 GB heap).


## 2026-05-13

- `convoy_fpgrowth.py`: default `--density-cap` 10000 → **2000**. Why: data-driven from `scripts/density_stats.py` — largest legitimate bucket observed is 1,718 plates (loc 16); 2000 leaves ~15% headroom and cleanly excludes the 23,595-plate freak bucket at loc 31 that caused the previous OOM.

- Density investigation (`scripts/density_stats.py`, polars). Per-location bucket-size distribution for windows {300, 200, 100, 50}s. Real max bucket = 1,718 plates (loc 16). One outlier of **23,595** at loc 31 whose own p99 is 1,297 — almost certainly a stuck camera or data artifact.

- Picked `density_cap = 2000` for the 300 s convoy run. Why: excludes the single 23 k outlier (the OOM trigger) while preserving every legitimate busy-intersection bucket. The earlier 500 guess was wrong — it would have thrown away ~half the buckets at the six busiest locations.

- First convoy run OOM'd in `FPGrowth.scala:274` (conditional FP-tree build). Cgroup-killed executor → shuffle map files vanish → `MetadataFetchFailedException`. Root cause was a single mega-bucket; `density_cap=10000` (inherited from the chunked legacy code) let it through.

- `convoy_fpgrowth.py` (`src/job/`): FP-Growth-based convoy miner. Drops the legacy iterative time-chunking and aggressive plate pre-filter (no longer needed at 90 cores / 375 GB executor RAM). Keeps the Grid A + Grid B (offset = window/2) trick for bucket-boundary smoothing — that's data physics, not a hardware workaround.

- `requirements.txt` baked into the image (numpy + pandas + pyarrow). Why: `pyspark.ml` hard-imports numpy at module load — the convoy job crashed immediately on `import` before our code even ran.


## 2026-05-12

- Five-node Spark standalone cluster ran cleanly under Slurm. basic-stats job: 275,893,209 rows / 22,058,054 plates / 977 locations, 31.00 days span. ~17 s end-to-end. Cluster, GPFS reads, FP-merge healthy.

- Uploaded `data/input/31.csv` (5.8 GB) via Windows → Linux through the platform's rtunnel proxy. Used `sftp -b - … <<< reput` for resume-on-drop after rsync hit two-end install + path-translation walls.

- Tuned resources to 5 × 20 cores × 100 GB nodes: EXECUTOR_CORES=18, EXECUTOR_MEMORY=75 g, DRIVER_MEMORY=12 g, SPARK_WORKER_MEMORY=85 g (worker advertises ≥ executor + headroom).

- Image build pipeline finalized: `src/image/{install-spark.sh,Dockerfile, requirements.txt}` install JDK 17 + Spark 3.5.3 (Hadoop-3 bundle) + the Python deps into `/opt`. Idempotent; health-checked via "binary runs" not just "binary exists".

- Hardcoded `source /inspire/.../src/env.sh` everywhere (no more $(dirname "$0") dance). Repo lives at a stable GPFS path; the indirection bought us nothing.


## 2026-05-11 — Architecture pivot

- Dropped Hadoop / HDFS / YARN from the original plan. Why: GPFS is already the shared filesystem ("free HDFS-equivalent"); Spark reads `file:///inspire/...` directly. No NameNode/DataNode, no YARN. Removes ~80 % of the original `cluster_launch.sh` from PLAN.md and sidesteps every replication/inode-limit constraint in reports.md.

- Confirmed Spark + Slurm coexist (no conflict). Slurm allocates containers; inside the allocation we bring up a Spark standalone master + workers. Pattern documented at Princeton, TACC, NIH-HPC, etc.

- Master discovery via GPFS file (`$CLUSTER_DIR/master.endpoint`), not DNS. Why: pod IPs are dynamic; DNS for k8s-ephemeral hostnames is unreliable; a shared FS we already have works for free.

- Pinned every Spark daemon to its container's primary intranet IP via `SPARK_LOCAL_IP=$(hostname -I)`. Required for driver↔executor traffic under the overlay network.


## Base — inherited state

- `base/reports.md`, `PLAN.md`, `base/PJ_AccompanyCars/codes/`: prior project targeting a 5-node × 8-core × 8 GB cluster with HDFS + YARN. Algorithmic core (Grid A/B sliding windows, FP-Growth on (location, bucket) transactions) carried forward; resource-constrained engineering (iterative chunking, aggressive plate pre-filter, density_cap=10 k) replaced.

- Dataset: 275.9 M records / 22 M unique plates / 977 locations / 31 days. Schema: `plate STRING, location STRING, timestamp BIGINT` (unix s).
